 ## Overview
-The Improved Quadratic Taylor Inclusion Function is a method for computing tighter, valid bounds on a scalar field function $f(t)$ along a ray, used to determine safe step sizes in sphere tracing. It solves the problem where standard quadratic bounds violate the first-order Lipschitz constraint, causing the bound to grow too fast and result in smaller, inefficient step sizes than linear bounds. It sets itself apart by enforcing both first-order ($\lambda$) and second-order ($\gamma$) Lipschitz constraints simultaneously, switching to a linear bound when the quadratic derivative exceeds the first-order limit [S1].
